A||N||10||mrossum||88 suspension into 86||||3||
Z||000000||mrossum||07-09-2001||11:38 PM||||i am getting ready to try to swap all of the suspension from an 88 into my 86. tried to do archive search, but didn't really get any info that i didn't already know. anyone already done this swap have any pictures of it so i can get it right the first time??||||reg||3
Z||000001||mrfiero||07-10-2001||12:10 AM||||It's not reasonably possible to do that swap.  The '88 rear cradle & suspension can be fairly easily adapted, but you must use an adapter kit and coilovers (Held Motorsports sells them).  <P>The front suspension, however, is not a simple bolt on.  You can install one if you are good with a welder, etc., but the amount of work needed to do it outweighs the relative expense of just buying an '88 to begin with ('88 coupes can be had for as little as a few hundred $$).<P>I personally wouldn't even attempt doing this (at least the front).....way too much work is involved.   ||||reg||1
Z||000002||TONY_C||07-10-2001||08:51 AM||||The 88 cradle can be installed into a 84-87 Fiero but it is not easy. I am in the middle of such a swap right now. It involves relocating the mounting holes for the struts which means that the existing strut towers need to be modified to clear the 88 struts. Yes, you can buy some adapters from aftermarket sources but they require the use of coilovers and they do not place the coilovers in the same exact location as the struts would be in the 88 suspension. To do the mods I have described you will need some welding experience and there is some extensive reworking (cutting and welding) to the strut towers. I would not attempt to do this conversion just for the sake of upgrading an older model to an 88 suspension. I am in the middle of a longitudinal V8 conversion and since I am cutting and hacking anyway it seemed to make sense to go with the later model cradle. As for the front suspension, I really couldn't tell you much about that althougt I would think that it would be quite difficult and probably not worth the effort as the real culprit to the 84-87 handling was the rear suspension and it's associated bump-steer problems.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||07-10-2001||03:59 PM||||The '88 rear cradle will bolt directly into the early chassis. The strut adapter plates from Held Motorsports put the tops of the struts in the same location as the stock '88 setup, which means about and inch in and 1/4 inch back. Moving the inch inboard is what necessitates going to coilovers, because these can be used closer to the wall of the strut tower.<BR>No fabrication necessary.<P><BR>The '88 front is rather more difficult, and requires cutting and welding.<BR>I assume you have found my post in the archives; I'm sorry, but I don't have any pics.<BR>If you haven't found my post in the archives, go search for it!||||reg||1
Z||000004||montage||07-12-2001||02:02 PM||||I just talked with George Miller at the Fiero Shop in Ca and he told me they are in the process of making a kit to put a 88 suspension on a pre-88 Fiero. Contact him to find out more, since I have an 88 I didn't get into too much detail with him.||||reg||1
Z||000005||honegod||12-13-2001||10:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>If you haven't found my post in the archives, go search for it!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I just spent a fun hour or so searching for your post, with no notable success.<BR>could you give me a hint or three ?<BR>my search skills are pathetic.<P>I have an 84 that I am looking at the 88 rear swap for.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadero2dmax||12-13-2001||11:55 PM||||I have an '88 suspension in my street daily driver SE (4.9), am putting one in my Challange car, had one in my former Q4 racer, etc.<P>It bolts in with the exception of haqving to use 2 1/2" springs and relocating the strut. As has been documented, a kit is available with an adapter from Held, less than a hundred bucks. The RCC coil-over is the cheapest way to go with a "coil-over" adjustable perch rear strut, but Ground Control, Held, and a host of others have just a kit you can use any strut to convert to a "coil-over" - - even your stockers!!<P>The cutting and welding that was mentioned on the front is that you have to cut the frame rails on both cars, the donor and the one being converted. The hardest part is getting the rails straight when you weld them back on - - a fixture is probably the best way to go.<P>But why?? The early chassis with '88 cradle is the best handling combo I have found after competing in Solo II, ProSolo, and track events in various Fieros for going on 13 years. Check out the photopoint album in my signature for my last Fiero, quad4 powered GT-2/Prepared (1900 LBS, 235HP, etc.) Fiero - with an '88 cradle.<P>I personally endorse your choice. <P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000007||honegod||12-14-2001||12:37 AM||||how about the brake hoses and cables ? <BR>do 88 rear hoses work with the 84 hardlines ?<BR>what parking brake cable setup is required ?<P>I asked mister steiger (fiero parts depot)if he would be willing to do the swap for me, he was not interested, saying that it was a very complicated and difficult operation.<BR>this from the guy who cheerfully swapped a 87 V6 & 5 speed into my 84, with all the attendant wiring and plumbing.<P>so I am all wondering what the hassle is.||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadero2dmax||12-14-2001||07:41 AM||||The brake hard lines are OK as is, you don't need to change them. You need to have longer brake hoses, but if you already have changed to Earl's braided then there is no problem (use the longer set for the rear). Since I put braided lines on my cars anyway - - -.<P>The parking brake cables are the stock '88 cables - as long as you use the stock '88 rear brakes. Every swap I have made I used that opportunity to change the brake cables anyway (I would consider that routine maintenance, sorta. I usually found the original cables were in pretty bad shape).<P>It takes a weekend to do the swap, about the same as any other cradle drop (engine swap or repair, tranny removal, clutch change, etc.)<P>This is not a difficult mod,the only costs are the "coil-over" adjustable perch struts, the adapters, and of course the cradle (try to get a Formula or GT - the coupe cradles don't have a rear swaybar and that could be an additional cost - -  if you want a rear swaybar - -).<P>Oh, this swap could be made to be expensive if you got all exotic, but there is a whole separate thread about the advantages of changing the rear struts to "coil-overs" on an otherwise stock suspension anyway. Sure, one can get a set of Koni adjustables for thier struts (kaching), and exotic links (kaching, kaching), Wilwood brakes (KACHING),  - in other words make it an expensive swap. But it need not be.<P>I wonder how the parts guy eliminates HIS bump steer?? The kits I have seen are only partially effective, and cost as much as an '88 cradle would in the first place. I'm sure the unwilling parts guy friend has SOME solution to bump steer in mind - - <P>I would invite anybody that hasn't driven an early chasis/late cradle to try it. There are certainly enough out there, it is a fairly common upgrade. Once you have experienced the smooth, predictable handling, you will be like me - - you will want this combo for life. <P>The early front suspension offers quicker turn-in,  better road feel, and smoother response than the '88. The '88 rear completely eliminates the bump steer problem, is bolted directly to the chassis (no thick rubber bushings to mush around) and is way more pliant. The rear suspension is what all the Lotus ad hype was all about - - the link type suspension is what both Lotus and Porshe use - - to this day!!<P>Can you tell I am sold on this mod? And if you browse the photos at the link in my signature, I am sure I won't need to list my credentials to make this recommendation   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to:  <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821</A>  <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||cadero2dmax||12-14-2001||07:48 AM||||Mrossum<P>I am putting an '88 cradle into the '85 Challenge car. It will be a month or so away before I get to that point, but photos will be posted in a gallery at: <P>GrassrootsChallengeFiero@yahoogroups.com<P>when I get to that point. In the interim, if I can answer any questions, please drop me a PM or e-mail me at:<P>gryan3@mindspring.com<P>G||||reg||1
